    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d131-a2">

                <pb facs="0218" n="212" xml:id="pg212"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.—Instructions No. 20.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hippesley to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Grafton Hotel, Connecticut Avenue</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1900-11-23"><hi rendition="#italic">November
                                23, 1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir</hi>: At the invitation of the president
                    of the Johns Hopkins University I last week delivered an address on the revenue
                    system of China before that institution. The question of the amount of indemnity
                    to be exacted from China is now actively occupying the attention of the
                    plenipotentiaries in Peking; and as I foresee that the insistence on a sum
                    beyond China’s capabilities will jeopardize both the independence of that
                    country and the future good relations of the western powers among themselves, I
                    venture to send you that portion of my address which dealt with this question,
                    in the hope that the presentation of the actual facts and figures by one who has
                    been occupied with them for many years may help to confining the demands within
                    moderate and feasible limits.</p>
                <p>I remain, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Alfred E.
                    Hippesley</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
